<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nunavut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nunavut is the largest and northernmost territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, created on 1 April 1999 from the eastern part of the Northwest Territories under the Nunavut Land Claims Agreement of 1993. Most of its roughly 40,000 people are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>, and Inuktitut is an official language alongside English and French. Its capital is Iqaluit on Baffin Island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nunavut.jpg" alt="Nunavut" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kooma (original) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nunavut.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nunavut covers 1,836,993.78 square kilometres of land and 160,930 square kilometres of water in Northern Canada. It includes a substantial part of the mainland, most of the Arctic Archipelago, and the waters and islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a>, James Bay and Ungava Bay, among them Ellesmere Island in the far north, parts of Victoria Island and the distant Belcher Islands and Akimiski Island. The highest point is Barbeau Peak, at 2,616 metres, on Ellesmere Island. Its boundary with the Northwest Territories roughly follows the tree line. Most regions have a polar climate, and the more southerly continental areas a very cold subarctic one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mainland was first populated about 4,500 years ago by the Pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, a Paleo-Eskimo culture that moved east from the Bering Strait region, and the Dorset culture, which developed from it, followed about 2,800 years ago. The Thule people, ancestors of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>, began to migrate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> in the 11th century, and by 1300 their settlement covered most of modern Nunavut. They replaced the Dorset genetically and culturally after 1300. The earliest written account of the area dates from 1576, when the English explorer Martin Frobisher, seeking the Northwest Passage, made the first recorded European contact with the Inuit in what is now Frobisher Bay. Henry Hudson, William Baffin and Robert Bylot followed in the 17th century.</p><p>In the 1950s, as part of the High Arctic relocation, the federal government moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> from Nunavik, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, to Resolute and Grise Fiord, where they faced starvation but were forced to stay. A 1994 report led to compensation. Debate on dividing the Northwest Territories along ethnic lines began in the 1950s, and a plebiscite on division was held on 14 April 1982. The land claims agreement was completed in September 1992 and ratified by nearly 85% of voters in Nunavut. Parliament passed the Nunavut Act and the Nunavut Land Claims Agreement Act on 9 July 1993, and the territory was established on 1 April 1999, the first major change to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s political map since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> joined in 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nunavut's capital is Iqaluit, on Baffin Island, chosen in a plebiscite in 1995. Other major communities are Rankin Inlet and Cambridge Bay. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 36,858 people, of whom 84.3% identified as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>. The official languages are the Inuit language, Inuktut, which includes Inuktitut and Inuinnaqtun, English and French. Alert, on Ellesmere Island, is the world's northernmost continuously inhabited place. Nunavut is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s only geopolitical region not connected to the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> by the Pan-American Highway.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nunavut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The creation of <b>Nunavut</b> in 1999, modern land claims agreements and the recognition of Aboriginal and treaty rights in section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982, began to reshape relations with Indigenous peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The James Bay and Northern Quebec Agreement of 1975 set the pattern for land claims; the Constitution Act of 1982 recognised Inuit as Aboriginal peoples; the Inuvialuit Final Agreement followed in 1984; and the <b>Nunavut</b> Land Claims Agreement of 1993 led to the creation of Nunavut in 1999.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
